challenges.description:
Which approach correctly creates a typed custom error that can be caught and narrowed with instanceof?

  A)  const NetworkError = { message: 'network failed' }
  B)  type NetworkError = { message: string }
  C)  class NetworkError extends Error { constructor(msg: string) { super(msg); this.name = 'NetworkError' } }
  D)  const NetworkError = (msg: string) => new Error(msg)

What is the purpose of `never` in this exhaustive check?

  type Status = 'running' | 'stopped' | 'failed'

  function describe(s: Status): string {
    switch (s) {
      case 'running': return 'Service is up'
      case 'stopped': return 'Service is down'
      case 'failed':  return 'Service has crashed'
      default:
        const _exhaustive: never = s
        return _exhaustive
    }
  }

What makes this a discriminated union, and why is it better than a single Error class with optional fields?

  type AppError =
    | { kind: 'network'; statusCode: number; url: string }
    | { kind: 'timeout'; url: string; ms: number }
    | { kind: 'auth'; reason: string }
